{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 530,
    "dDurationMs": 1939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Want all juicy celebrity gossip?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2469,
    "dDurationMs": 4951,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Forget TMZ - Here on Space Time we have all\nthe latest details on the dysfunctional, explosive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7420,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relationships between the stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15200,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let me tell you a tale of a pair of star-crossed … well,\nstars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20690,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When our galaxy was a little younger there\nwere two ordinary stars - perhaps not unlike"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25560,
    "dDurationMs": 3820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "our sun, and they danced together in binary\norbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29380,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Romantic, right?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30740,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But any good romance is also a tragedy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33710,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After a billion or so years, one star died."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36960,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It had burned brighter and faster, until it’s\nheart of fusing hydrogen shriveled into a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42450,
    "dDurationMs": 1650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dead core of carbon and oxygen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44100,
    "dDurationMs": 5889,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ejecting its outer layers, it became a searing\nhot, planet-sized orb of incredible density"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49989,
    "dDurationMs": 2581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- a white dwarf."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52570,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is where the romantic tragedy turns\ninto a horror story."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57430,
    "dDurationMs": 4819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As the zombified stellar core and still-living\ncompanion spiraled closer together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62249,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A stream dull, red gas now connected the two\n- the outer envelope of the star falling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67140,
    "dDurationMs": 4019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into the intense gravitational embrace of\nits old companion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71159,
    "dDurationMs": 4841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There, in the extreme surface gravity of the\nultradense white dwarf, a layer of hydrogen"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76000,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "built up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 77000,
    "dDurationMs": 4030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At a critical point, that surface reached\nthe temperature and pressure of a stellar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 81030,
    "dDurationMs": 1110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82140,
    "dDurationMs": 4519,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A storm of fusion ripped around the planet-sized\nwhite dwarf, spraying its atmosphere into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86659,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space and for a couple of weeks shining 10s\nof thousands of times brighter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91950,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Centuries later, on March 11, 1437, the light\nfrom that explosion swept past the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99430,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There, the royal astronomers of King Sejong’s\ncourt in Korea recorded a new point of light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105890,
    "dDurationMs": 4689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the constellation of Wei, in what we call\nScorpius."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110579,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They named it a guest star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113469,
    "dDurationMs": 5321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We now call this phenomenon a nova, from stella\nnova, or new star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118790,
    "dDurationMs": 4109,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Romantic names, even if the stellar partnership\nis a disaster."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122899,
    "dDurationMs": 4591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our galaxy is full of these sorts of dysfunctional\nstellar relationships."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127490,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With more than half of all stars existing\nin binary orbits, it’s inevitable that many"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131600,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "stellar remnants will end up in these parasitic\nspirals with their partners."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136500,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to look at the worst of\nthese - from the novae produced by white dwarfs,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to X-ray binaries created by neutron stars\nand black holes - and much weirder things"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146760,
    "dDurationMs": 1309,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "besides."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 148069,
    "dDurationMs": 3691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days, if you point one of our newfangled\ngiant telescopes at the same spot where the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 151760,
    "dDurationMs": 3371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "royal Korean astronomers saw their guest star\n… you see nothing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155131,
    "dDurationMs": 2819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you pan a bit you find a puff of gas\n- a beautiful nebula, all that remains of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 157950,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that explosion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159200,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mysteriously, the ill-fated binary isn’t\nin the center of inside the nebula - it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162880,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wandered a bit since 1437."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165540,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it can be found if you look a little off center for a spot of light that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169380,
    "dDurationMs": 3210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flares erratically\nfrom visible to X-ray wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172590,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was Mike Shara Astrophysics Curator at the American\nMuseum of Natural History, who figured all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178260,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of this out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179260,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After discovering the nebula from the 1437\nnova back in the 80s, he spent decades tracking"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185720,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "down the culprit system."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187810,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He finally identified a nearby flaring white\ndwarf binary - a so-called cataclysmic variable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194030,
    "dDurationMs": 6530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- and realized that it was the same object\nas a dwarf nova that he found on multiple"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200560,
    "dDurationMs": 4509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "old photographic places as far back as 1923."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205069,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A dwarf nova is what you think - it's like a regular\nnova, but much weaker."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210849,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They result when denser streams of matter\nhit the white dwarf and flare due to heat,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216769,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but do not produce the storm of fusion of\nthe classical nova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220709,
    "dDurationMs": 6371,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But as Dr Shara discovered, it turns out that\ndwarf novae are just what classical nova do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227080,
    "dDurationMs": 2290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between those bigger explosions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229370,
    "dDurationMs": 5210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With observations of this dwarf nova spanning\nthe last century, Shara could extrapolate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234580,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its path back another half-millenium."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 237390,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That placed it exactly where those royal astronomers\nsaw their classical nova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242810,
    "dDurationMs": 5659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So cataclysmic variables must slowly build\nup their hydrogen layer, sputtering and flaring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248469,
    "dDurationMs": 5431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as they do so, until a critical temperature\nand pressure sends them over the edge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253900,
    "dDurationMs": 3630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "After which they start the whole process all over again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257530,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The system responsible for the 1437 nova is\nby no means unusual."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261820,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "50 or so classical novae go off in our galaxy\nevery year."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266320,
    "dDurationMs": 5659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cataclysmic variables do come in some variety\n- for example we have polars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271979,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the white dwarf has a strong magnetic field,\nthe flow of gas from its companion is channeled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276889,
    "dDurationMs": 1201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by that field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278090,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As charged particles spiral along the magnetic\nfield lines they emit synchrotron radiation,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283180,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and bright X-ray light is emitted as the gas\nhits the polar regions of the white dwarf"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287480,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- like a particularly violent auroras."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291470,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cataclysmic variables are somewhat impressive,\nbut for a real cataclysm it’s hard to go"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296770,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "past an X-ray binary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299170,
    "dDurationMs": 3510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just replace the white dwarf with a neutron\nstar or black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 302680,
    "dDurationMs": 3060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those are what you get when the most massive\nstars die."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305740,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The remnant core now contracts to the point\nthat atomic nuclei are no longer distinct"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310210,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- instead they meld together, protons and\nelectrons combine to become neutrons, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314970,
    "dDurationMs": 3669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you’re left with a ball of hyperdense matter\nthe size of a city."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318639,
    "dDurationMs": 5191,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And its mass is high enough it sucks itself\ninto a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323830,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is all stuff we’ve talked about before\n- be we haven’t seen the effect on a hapless"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 329020,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "companion star of having one of these as its\nbinary partner."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333240,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Once again, if the two are close enough, gas\nis syphoned from the star onto the black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338900,
    "dDurationMs": 1370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340270,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If forms an accretion disk - and in X-ray\nbinaries, it’s the accretion disk itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345810,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that glows bright."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347090,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s because the gravitational field of\nthe compact object is so strong, falling gas"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351280,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "reaches incredible speeds - which means incredible\nfriction - which means heat and light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356450,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They glow X-ray hot."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358580,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And like cataclysmic variables, the flow is\nuneven so the X-rays fluctuate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363840,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of neutron star X-ray binaries,\nthat fluctuation includes powerful flares,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368840,
    "dDurationMs": 4910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "resulting from denser clumps of material hitting\nthe rapidly rotating surface of the neutron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373750,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374750,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sometimes we also see the neutron star as\na pulsar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 376770,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Its powerful magnetic field channels high\nenergy particles into a jet that traces a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 382040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "circle across the sky - and often sweeping\npast the earth to produce metronome-precise"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 386840,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pulses - most brightly in radio light, but\npotentially at all wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393380,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Black hole x-ray binaries seem a bit more\nboring by comparison, because the black hole has"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398400,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "no surface for the gas to fall onto - so no x-ray flares."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402330,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The nearest such system is the famous Cynus\nX1 X-ray binary, where a black hole the mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407490,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of 15 Suns is busy gorging on a blue giant\nstar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412610,
    "dDurationMs": 4451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As with cataclysmic variables, X-ray binaries\nare relatively common - we know of 100s in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 417061,
    "dDurationMs": 1779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418840,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are some much rarer, and, frankly, more awful manifestations of this phenomenon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424300,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Take the black widow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425440,
    "dDurationMs": 3759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is almost as cool a detective story as\nthe 1437 nova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429199,
    "dDurationMs": 5101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To start, you need to know that when you look\nat our galaxy in gamma rays - the highest"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434300,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy light there is - the brightest points\nyou see are pulsars, and those gamma ray spots"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439560,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are pretty much always accompanied by the\nclassic metronome-precise pulses of radio"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444610,
    "dDurationMs": 1399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446009,
    "dDurationMs": 2581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Except  of course when they’re not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448590,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are a handful of mysteriously pulse-free\ngamma ray sources that otherwise look like"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454110,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they should be pulsars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455110,
    "dDurationMs": 3140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was Roger Romani of Stanford who figured\nthis one out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458250,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He observed these objects using visible wavelength\nof light - and found one object was indeed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 463270,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pulsing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 464270,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the pulses were far too slow - it brightened\nand dimmed avery … hours, while pulsars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470300,
    "dDurationMs": 3450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flash on the scale of seconds, or even microseconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473750,
    "dDurationMs": 4259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The source also become bluer as it brightened,\nredder as it faded."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478009,
    "dDurationMs": 5591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well it turns out this object is a pulsar, and\nit’s in orbit around a companion star - in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483600,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this case a brown dwarf, which is a star not\nquite massive enough to generate its own energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488780,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by nuclear fusion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 490540,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In this case the companion didn’t start\nout as a brown dwarf - it became one after"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495220,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "losing most of its mass to its ravenous partner."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498220,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That brown dwarf orbits perilously close\nto the neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503040,
    "dDurationMs": 5839,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The neutron star’s jets sweep it hundreds\nof times per second, slowly blasting away"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508879,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its gas."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509879,
    "dDurationMs": 4581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That gas forms an enveloping ring around the\nwhole system, which then falls onto the neutron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514460,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515460,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The same gas blocks any radio light, but allows\nthe more penetrating gamma ray light to pass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521030,
    "dDurationMs": 1330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 522360,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And the pulsing of visible light?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524720,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that’s when the super-heated “daytime” side of the brown dwarf comes into our view,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530390,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the red, dim phase is when we are looking\nat its night side."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534260,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This object became known as the black widow,\nand with the discovery of several similar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539550,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "systems, black widow is now the name of the\nobject class."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 544590,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sticking with the deadly spider motif, if that second star is a red dwarf we have a red back."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 549960,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That second star is doomed to an ignominious end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554300,
    "dDurationMs": 6620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First its whittled away until its not a\nstar any more, and eventually we expect it to become"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560920,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to become more and more Jupiter-like  and then\njust an icy core."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565520,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Finally that core is expected to break up\nin the neutron star’s tidal field and be"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569710,
    "dDurationMs": 1550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scattered into the void."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571260,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand cataclysmic variables - like the one that produced the 1437 nova - have a more impressive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576920,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end to look forward to."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578580,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The white dwarf in these systems builds up\nmass until releasing it as a nova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582860,
    "dDurationMs": 4990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in that explosion it only ejects maybe\n5% of the accreted material."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 587850,
    "dDurationMs": 3410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The rest stays with the white dwarf, which\nslowly grows in mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591260,
    "dDurationMs": 3350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Eventually, the core of the white dwarf reaches a temperature of hundred of millions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 594610,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Kelvin, and the star’s carbon and oxygen\ncan begin to fuse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599250,
    "dDurationMs": 6170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A runaway fusion reaction rips through the\nstar, which explodes as a Type 1 supernova."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605420,
    "dDurationMs": 2800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Those supernovae are visible not just across the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608220,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "galaxy, but in galaxies across the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610350,
    "dDurationMs": 7350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To them, they were the explosives ends to long and\nfiery relationships, but to us they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617700,
    "dDurationMs": 7860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seem a little petulant. Like the final slamming of doors from distant parts of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625560,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As you know, at the start of the pandemic\nwe all had to quarantine on Earth to avoid contaminating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630980,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space with the virus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632510,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, after several months shooting in my\napartment we finally managed to develop protocols"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637170,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to protect the universe from Earthly lurgies\nand so here I am, floating in the void once"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 642700,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 643700,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So for this comment response we’re going\nback a few weeks to our episode on this strange"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 648440,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new observation by LIGO: gravitational waves\nfrom the merger of a black hole with ... something"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653510,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "else."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654670,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Something that seemed to straddle the mass\nbetween black holes and neutron stars, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658740,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which will change the way we think about whichever\nof those it turns out to be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 662780,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s get to the questions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664460,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zack Hamburg asks how we know that a black\nhole isn’t just a neutron star behind an"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669240,
    "dDurationMs": 1060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670300,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Why should the star have crushed down into\na point-like singularity at all?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674060,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To give everyone some context: When a massive\nstar dies, its core becomes a neutron star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678350,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but if that core is above a certain mass\nit shrinks so that the escape velocity at"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682800,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its surface is greater than the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685120,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s when it becomes a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687060,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what happens to the neutron star after\nit collapses enough to form an event horizon?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692840,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, below that event horizon, we can think\nof space flowing downwards faster than the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698310,
    "dDurationMs": 1190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699500,
    "dDurationMs": 3620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means the neutron star has no choice\nbut to contract until no more contraction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703120,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is possible - when it has zero size."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705990,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This conclusion is unavoidable if you’re\nonly using general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710030,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We think a theory of quantum gravity probably\nprevents the singularity from really forming"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714310,
    "dDurationMs": 5850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- but quantum gravity effects would not kick\nin soon enough to save the neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 720160,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Catinboots81 and Vivallamannen asks whether\nthe strange smaller object in the merger might"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725550,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have been a primordial black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728710,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Good insight there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 730010,
    "dDurationMs": 2570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dead stars aren’t the only way to make black\nholes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732580,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some black holes may have formed from the\nextremely dense matter of the early universe,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737180,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and these would have different mass restrictions\nthan stellar black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741060,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact, people have considered primordial\nblack holes as an explanation for other LIGO"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745570,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mergers - which often involve black holes\nMORE massive than was thought normal for stellar"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 750260,
    "dDurationMs": 1930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remnants could create."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752190,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’s also possible that primordial black\nholes could be less massive than black holes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756070,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that come from stars, so might explain this\nweird teensy possible black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760770,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Interestingly, this should be testable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764010,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If primordial black holes exist in some abundance\nat these masses, then the universe should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769530,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be very faintly humming with a gravitational\nwave background from the countless mergers"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 775130,
    "dDurationMs": 1790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than happened in the earlier universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 776920,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "LIGO hasn’t seen that background yet - which\nactually limits how many such primordial black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781920,
    "dDurationMs": 1450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes there might be."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 783370,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If LIGO continues to fail to detect this background\nthen it’ll become less and less likely that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788200,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "primordial black holes are responsible for\nany LIGO events."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792120,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some of you also asked why the less massive object\ncan't just be a regular star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795880,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's an easy one - in order to generate\ndetectible gravitational waves, both objects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800180,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "need to be extremely compact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802120,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The waves get generated when extreme masses\nspiral together at very small distances."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807180,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Compared to a black hole or neutron star,\nregular stars are giant puffed up balls."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812990,
    "dDurationMs": 4390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They are ripped apart before getting close\nenough to generate gravitational waves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817380,
    "dDurationMs": 3970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Frank and Jim asked how the event horizons\nof merging black holes change just before"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821350,
    "dDurationMs": 1360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they combine."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 822710,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You guys have it right - they do deform into\na sort of 8 or hourglass shape - in the sense"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827310,
    "dDurationMs": 4810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that the event horizons sort of reach out\nto each other, connect, before coming together"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832120,
    "dDurationMs": 4650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into an ovoid and then finally a sphere, or\nflattened sphere for a rotating black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 836770,
    "dDurationMs": 3870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Note that this doesn’t say anything about\nthe shape of the stuff inside the black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 840640,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- we’re just the event horizon - the surface\nbelow which there’s a faster-than-light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845730,
    "dDurationMs": 2020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flow of space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847750,
    "dDurationMs": 6630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s a simulation from the SXS - simulating\nextreme spacetimes group at ... that shows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854380,
    "dDurationMs": 2450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how the event horizons merge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856830,
    "dDurationMs": 8590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Military Archive says finally here is an episode\nI can kind of understand. Well Mr. Archive, maybe the reason episodes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865420,
    "dDurationMs": 4150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get easier is that you’re getting smarter\nwith all that Space Time you’re watching."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 869570,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or maybe I’m just getting better at explaining\nstuff."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872750,
    "dDurationMs": 1950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Can we ever know?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874700,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Laura Chapple follows up by saying this episode\nwas worryingly comprehensible and wonders"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880150,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we’ve run out of physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 881970,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, as Einstein said: The eternal mystery\nof the world is its comprehensibility."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 886810,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you’re comprehending Space Time does\nthat make you Einstein?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890940,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can test that - we have many ideas for\nepisodes that are currently totally incomprehensible,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896560,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at least to me."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 898520,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see how far we can go."
    } ]
  } ]
}
